before:
```typescript
type Signal = {kind:"on"} | {kind:"off"};
function signalText(signal: Signal): string {
  switch (signal.kind) {
    case "on": return "Enabled";
    case "off": return "Disabled";
  }
}
```

An assertNever helper can also throw if invalid data unexpectedly reaches that branch at runtime. It does not replace validation of outside data.

Here the helper and its call appear together:
```typescript
type Signal = {kind:"on"} | {kind:"off"};
function assertNever(value: never): never {
  throw new Error("Unexpected variant");
}
function signalText(signal: Signal): string {
  switch (signal.kind) {
    case "on": return "Enabled";
    case "off": return "Disabled";
    default: return assertNever(signal);
  }
}
console.log(signalText({kind:"on"}));
```
This prints Enabled. assertNever accepts only a value narrowed to never and never returns normally. A valid handled variant never reaches that call. Adding {kind:"pause"} to Signal without adding its case makes the default call a type error: pause remains possible there. That changed program is supposed to fail compilation, not print a result.

after:
The final helper call catches a forgotten case during checking.
```typescript
type Signal = { kind: "on" } | { kind: "off" };
function assertNever(value: never): never {
  throw new Error("Unexpected variant");
}
function signalText(signal: Signal): string {
  switch (signal.kind) {
    case "on": return "Enabled";
    case "off": return "Disabled";
    default: return assertNever(signal);
  }
}
console.log(signalText({ kind: "on" }));
```
The output is Enabled. If a new kind is added without a case, the final call becomes a type error.
